<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/advanced/art-market-and-museums">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The art market and its museums are <strong>the economics and governance lessons wearing white
    gloves</strong>: a unique asset class, a reputation economy, and the institutions that decide
    which art becomes History — which is also, increasingly, which art becomes expensive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="anomalous assets, the primary build, the secondary theatre, museums and power" widget="equationStep" config={marketCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the system honestly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Provenance is both ethics and title law:</strong> ownership history decides restitution claims (the beyond-the-West lesson\'s looted works), resale rights (droit de suite in Europe), and authenticity context — the market\'s memory is its legal system (the international-law lesson\'s treaties, art-napping edition: the 1970 UNESCO Convention is the border).</li>
    <li><strong>Price ≠ quality, provably:</strong> the market rewards scarcity, fashion and the canon\'s machinery — women artists\' prices vs their museum representation measure the bias precisely (the gender lesson\'s data, market-priced); "undervalued" is an empirical claim here.</li>
    <li><strong>Money launderers love freeports:</strong> opaque high-value movable assets attract exactly what you\'d expect — regulation (the EU\'s anti-money-laundering rules covering art, 2020s) is the law lesson catching up with the market lesson.</li>
    <li><strong>The NFT moment as a case study:</strong> scarcity engineering on digital files — a real experiment in what the market lesson prices (provenance? status? the certificate?) that crashed on the fundamentals (the behavioural-finance lesson\'s bubbles, at browser speed).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Velthuis, Talking Prices; Thompson, The $12 Million Stuffed Shark; the economics and governance lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
